.TH LN 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
ln \- make hard links or symbolic links
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ln
.RI [ options ]
.I target link
.br
.B ln
.RI [ options ]
.IR target ...\&
.I directory
.br
.B ln
.RI [ options ]
.I target
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ln
makes a link named
.I link
to
.IR target .
By default the link is a hard link, another name for the same file.
With
.BR \-s ,
it is a symbolic link, a small file that holds a path to
.IR target .
This
.B ln
is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR ln ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Operands
With one operand,
.B ln
makes the link in the current directory, named after the last component
of
.IR target ,
so that
.B ln ../lib/util.h
makes
.IR util.h .
That name is then treated as the
.I link
operand below, so if a directory of that name exists, the link is made
inside it.
.PP
With more operands, the last is
.IR link .
If it is an existing directory, or a symbolic link or junction to one,
each
.I target
is linked into it, under the last component of the
.IR target .
Otherwise
.I link
is the new name, and only one
.I target
is expected.
.B ln
does not check this: it links each
.I target
to the same name in turn.
The first makes the link, and each other fails with
.BR "File exists" .
With
.BR \-f ,
each replaces the one before, so the last wins.
.PP
The last component of a
.I target
is found after removing trailing slashes and backslashes.
A name that is already taken is an error, unless
.B \-f
or
.B \-b
is given.
Each
.I target
is processed on its own: an error is reported, and the remaining
targets are still linked.
.SS Hard links
.I target
must exist, though a symbolic link whose own target is missing counts as
existing.
It cannot be a directory.
The file system must support hard links, and
.I link
must be on the same volume.
All names of a file share its contents and attributes, such as read-only.
.SS Symbolic links
With
.BR \-s ,
.I target
need not exist.
It is stored as written, except that slashes become backslashes.
Windows resolves a relative
.I target
from the directory that holds
.IR link ,
not from the current directory, so
.B ln \-s src/a.c build
makes
.I build/a.c
point to
.IR build/src/a.c .
.PP
Windows has file links and directory links.
.B ln
makes a directory link when
.I target
is an existing directory at that moment, looked up where Windows will
resolve it, and a file link otherwise, even if
.I target
does not exist yet.
Windows does not follow a file link as a directory, so make the link
again after creating the directory.
.SS Output
.B ln
writes nothing, except with
.BR \-v .
It reports each failure to make a link on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ln: \fIname\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I name
is the link that could not be made, or a hard link
.I target
that does not exist.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-sf ,
and may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
The argument of
.B \-S
may be attached, as in
.BR \-S.bak ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options.
.TP
.B \-s
Make symbolic links instead of hard links.
Windows allows this only in Developer Mode or with the privilege to
create symbolic links; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B \-f
Delete an existing
.I link
first.
A read-only file is deleted too, after its read-only attribute is
cleared, which every hard link to the file shares.
A failure to delete is not reported, and the new link then fails if the
name is still taken.
.IP
The deletion comes before the new link is made, so the old
.I link
is lost if that fails, as it does when symbolic links are not permitted.
.B ln
does not check that
.I link
and
.I target
are the same file, so
.B ln \-f x x
deletes
.IR x .
For a hard link, a
.I target
that does not exist is found first, and nothing is deleted.
.TP
.B \-n
When
.I link
is a symbolic link or junction to a directory, treat it as an ordinary
name instead of linking into the directory.
With
.BR \-f ,
the symbolic link or junction itself is deleted and replaced, and the
directory is left alone.
This has no effect when
.I link
is itself a directory.
.B \-T
implies it.
.TP
.B \-b
Before making the link, rename an existing
.I link
to its name followed by a suffix, which is
.B ~
unless
.B \-S
gives another.
An existing file with the backup name is replaced.
If
.I link
does not exist, nothing is renamed and that is not an error.
Any other failure to rename is reported, and the
.I target
is skipped.
Overrides
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BI "\-S " suffix
Use
.I suffix
instead of
.B ~
for the name of a backup.
This does not make backups by itself.
.TP
.B \-T
Treat
.I link
as the name of the link, never as a directory to link into.
A symbolic link or junction to a directory is not followed, as with
.BR \-n ,
so it counts as an existing name.
A real directory is an error, and so are more than two operands.
.TP
.B \-v
Write
.BI \(aq link "\(aq \-> \(aq" target \(aq
on standard output for each link before it is made, so links that then
fail are written too.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0.
The message lists a
.B \-t
option, which
.B ln
does not accept.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows shows its critical-error dialogs, such as for a drive with no
disk in it, instead of the operation failing with an error.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR CONEMUPID ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every link was made.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error, an
error with
.BR \-T ,
or any link that could not be made.
.SH EXAMPLES
Keep a second name for a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ln data.txt backup.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Link two programs into a directory, listing each link:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ln \-v a.exe b.exe bin
\(aqbin/a.exe\(aq \-> \(aqa.exe\(aq
\(aqbin/b.exe\(aq \-> \(aqb.exe\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Link a header into the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ln ../common/util.h
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a symbolic link.
The target is relative to the directory that holds the link:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ln \-s ../lib/libfoo.dll bin/libfoo.dll
.EE
.RE
.PP
Point a symbolic link to a different directory, replacing the link
instead of linking into its old target:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ln \-sfn gcc\-15 current
.EE
.RE
.PP
Replace a file with a link to another, keeping the old file as
.IR app.cfg.bak :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ln \-b \-S .bak app.new app.cfg
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR ln :
.IP \(bu 2
Long options, such as
.BR \-\-backup ,
and the options
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-F ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-r ,
and
.B \-t
are invalid.
.IP \(bu 2
The same file is not detected.
.B ln \-f x x
deletes
.IR x ,
and so does
.B ln \-f x
with one operand.
.B ln \-b x x
renames
.I x
to
.IR x~ ,
and then fails.
Windows file names are not case sensitive by default, so
.B ln \-f x X
also deletes
.IR x .
.IP \(bu 2
Several targets are not checked to have a directory as
.IR link ;
see
.BR Operands .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-v
writes each line before the link is made.
.PP
Hard links need no privilege, but a directory cannot be hard linked:
that fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
Creating a symbolic link needs Developer Mode or the privilege to create
symbolic links, which an elevated administrator has, and otherwise fails
with
.BR "Permission denied" .
Windows XP has no symbolic links, so there
.B \-s
always fails.
With
.B \-f
or
.BR \-b ,
the old link is then already deleted or renamed.
.PP
A junction, as made by
.BR jn (1),
is followed like a symbolic link to decide whether
.I link
is a directory, and
.B \-n
and
.B \-T
treat it as an ordinary name.
.B ln
cannot make junctions.
.PP
Paths may have drive letters and backslashes.
Names in a directory are joined with a slash, so
.B ln \-v x \(aqdir\e\(aq
writes
.BR \(aqdir\e/x\(aq .
A name made from a
.I target
is what follows its last slash, backslash, or drive prefix.
A bare drive such as
.I c:
names the current directory of that drive, so
.B ln x c:
makes
.IR c:x .
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
A full path of 260 or more characters, counting the current directory
for a relative path, fails.
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B ln
expands wildcards in its arguments itself; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR jn (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR readlink (1)
